CAT = "phpsyEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("Under R.A. 10029, a psychometrician may administer, score and interpret which kind of test?",
 "Objective personality tests", ["Projective tests", "Inkblot techniques", "Picture-story techniques"],
 "The Psychology Act lets psychometricians handle objective and structured personality tests, excluding projective tests. Projective tests are for trained psychologists."),
("Under R.A. 10029, a psychometrician's assessment work must at all times be done under the supervision of:",
 "A licensed psychologist", ["A psychiatrist", "A guidance counselor", "The employer"],
 "The Act's definition of a psychometrician requires supervision by a licensed professional psychologist. A guidance counselor is licensed under a different law."),
("The Philippine Psychology Act of 2009 is:",
 "R.A. 10029", ["R.A. 11036", "R.A. 9258", "R.A. 10173"],
 "R.A. 10029 regulates psychologists and psychometricians. R.A. 9258 is the Guidance and Counseling Act, R.A. 11036 the Mental Health Act and R.A. 10173 the Data Privacy Act."),
("Under R.A. 10029, which task may a psychometrician do to prepare a client for psychological intervention?",
 "A preparatory intake interview", ["Individual psychotherapy sessions", "A formal diagnosis", "Prescribing treatment"],
 "The Act allows psychometricians to conduct preparatory intake interviews, under a psychologist's supervision. Diagnosis and psychotherapy belong to psychologists."),
("The Code of Ethics that governs Philippine psychometricians, adopted by the Board in 2017, was first adopted in 2008 by:",
 "The Psychological Association of the Philippines", ["The Department of Health", "The Commission on Higher Education", "The Philippine Mental Health Association"],
 "The PAP, the profession's accredited integrated professional organization, adopted the code in 2008; the Board adopted the reviewed code in 2017 (Resolution No. 11)."),
("Under the Code of Ethics, informed consent before an assessment may be skipped when the assessment is:",
 "Mandated by law", ["Requested by a parent", "Done online", "Short and simple"],
 "The Code lists exceptions: when mandated by law, when implied in routine institutional activity, or when the purpose is to assess decisional capacity. Length and format are not exceptions."),
("Under the Code of Ethics, which should a practitioner NOT release as test data?",
 "Raw scores and item responses", ["A summary of findings", "Recommendations", "A plain-language explanation of results"],
 "The Code says raw and scaled scores, the client's responses to items and notes on behavior during testing are not released. Findings are shared through the report."),
("Basing a job recommendation on an intelligence test the applicant took eight years ago goes against the Code's standard on:",
 "Outdated test results", ["Test construction", "Informed consent", "Test security"],
 "The Code says practitioners shall not base conclusions on outdated results or obsolete tests. Abilities and norms change over time."),
("Under the Code of Ethics, test manuals, answer keys and reusable booklets should be handled only by:",
 "Qualified users", ["The client", "Any office staff", "The referring employer"],
 "The test security standard limits test materials to qualified personnel. Leaked items lose their validity for everyone."),
("When explaining assessment results directly to a client with no background in psychology, the practitioner should use:",
 "Non-technical language", ["Raw score tables", "Technical terms", "Written scores only"],
 "The Code asks practitioners to explain results to clients, relatives, parents or teachers in non-technical language. Raw test data are not released."),
("A psychometrician is asked by a lawyer to interpret a Rorschach protocol for a court case. She should:",
 "Decline and refer", ["Interpret it carefully", "Score and report it", "Ask the court's permission"],
 "Projective tests are outside a psychometrician's scope under R.A. 10029 and the Code, so the case belongs with a trained psychologist."),
("Under the Code of Ethics, a practitioner may release confidential information without the client's consent mainly when:",
 "There is imminent danger", ["A relative asks for it", "The employer is curious", "A colleague is interested"],
 "The Code allows release to proper authorities when there is imminent danger to the person or the community, or when the law mandates it."),
("Before an assessment, clients should be told about the nature of the service, fees, potential risks and:",
 "Limits of confidentiality", ["The examiner's salary", "Expected test scores", "Other clients' results"],
 "The Code requires explaining the nature of services, financial arrangements, potential risks and the limits of confidentiality."),
("A practitioner's competence is not enough to serve a client's needs, and there is no emergency. The Code expects her to:",
 "Make an appropriate referral", ["Continue with extra care", "Have a peer co-sign the report", "Reduce her fee"],
 "Practitioners work within the boundaries of their competence and refer when it is not enough, except when providing services in emergencies."),
("Under the Code of Ethics, a practitioner may give services outside her competence when:",
 "It is an emergency with no other help", ["The client offers a higher fee", "The client insists on her", "Her supervising psychologist is on leave"],
 "In an emergency, when no mental health services are available, the Code allows help so the person is not deprived of care; it ends once proper help is reached."),
("A psychometrician is asked to test her own cousin for a company's hiring. The main ethical concern is:",
 "Loss of objectivity", ["Lower test reliability", "A ceiling effect", "Lack of norms"],
 "Testing a relative creates a multiple relationship that can bias scoring and interpretation. The test's reliability is unchanged."),
("Which Philippine law governs collecting, storing and sharing personal information such as assessment records?",
 "Data Privacy Act of 2012", ["Mental Health Act of 2018", "Cybercrime Prevention Act", "Magna Carta for Disabled Persons"],
 "The Data Privacy Act (R.A. 10173) covers processing of personal information. The Cybercrime Prevention Act covers offenses committed online."),
("Under the Data Privacy Act, a client's health and psychological records are classed as:",
 "Sensitive personal information", ["Public information", "Non-personal data", "Business records"],
 "Health information is sensitive personal information, which generally needs consent or another legal basis to process."),
("In a psychological report, the section that lists every instrument given is usually titled:",
 "Tests administered", ["Reason for referral", "Recommendations", "Behavioral observations"],
 "Reports list the tests administered so readers know the basis of the findings. The reason for referral states the question the assessment answers."),
("In a psychological report, notes on the examinee's effort, mood and attention during testing belong under:",
 "Behavioral observations", ["Tests administered", "Background information", "Summary of findings"],
 "Behavioral observations describe how the person acted during testing, which affects how much the scores can be trusted."),
("A good psychological report is organized mainly around:",
 "The referral question", ["The examiner's favorite theory", "Each test's raw scores", "The length of testing"],
 "A report should answer the question that prompted the assessment, integrating results rather than listing scores test by test."),
("Under R.A. 10029, practicing as a psychometrician without a valid registration may be punished by:",
 "A fine, imprisonment or both", ["A written warning only", "Community service in a barangay", "Loss of the right to vote"],
 "The Act's penal provisions allow a fine, imprisonment or both, at the court's discretion."),
("A practitioner needs an interpreter to assess a patient from another region. The Code requires her to ensure:",
 "Confidentiality and test security", ["A shorter version is used", "The family scores the test", "No written report is made"],
 "When a third-party interpreter is needed, the Code says the confidentiality of results and the security of the tests must be ensured."),
("A practitioner posts real items from a published intelligence test online to help applicants practice. This violates:",
 "Test security", ["Informed consent", "Content validity", "Rapport"],
 "Disclosing items breaks test security and makes scores of future examinees invalid. Only qualified users should handle test materials."),
]
